  "full_response": "Design patterns are reusable solutions to common problems in software design. They provide a template or preset solution that can be applied to specific problems, helping to make code more understandable, flexible, and maintainable. Design patterns are divided into three main categories: Creational, Structural, and Behavioral patterns. Here, we'll discuss the Observer, Factory, and Singleton patterns.\n\n1. **Observer Pattern**:\n   The Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern where an object (the subject or observable) maintains a list of dependents (observers) and notifies them of any state changes, usually by calling one of their methods. It establishes a one-to-many dependency between objects, allowing for objects to decouple and to be loosely coupled. This pattern is called an Observer pattern because the subjects and observers maintain lists of their counterparts and notify them about changes.\n\n   - **Subject (Observable)**: Holds the state in question, along with a list of its dependents, and notifies them when its state changes.\n   - **Observer**: An interface defining the methods that get called when the subject's state changes. implementations of a subject can maintain a list of observers and call them when the state changes.\n\n   Example in Python:\n   ```python\n   class Subject:\n       def __init__(self):\n           self._observers = []\n\n       def add_observer(self, observer):\n           self._observers.append(observer)\n\n       def remove_observer(self, observer):\n           self._observers.remove(observer)\n\n       def notify_observers(self):\n           for observer in self._observers:\n               observer.update()\n\n   class Observer:\n       def update(self):\n           raise NotImplementedError(\"This method should be overridden.\")\n\n   class ConcreteObserver(Observer):\n       def update(self):\n           print(\"Observer notifies that the state has changed.\")\n   ```\n\n2. **Factory Pattern**:\n   The Factory pattern is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating objects without specifying the exact class of object that will be created. It defines an interface for creating an object, but allows the instantiation to be deferred to subclasses. This pattern is useful when a system should not be dependent on the concrete classes of objects that it needs to work with, promoting loose coupling and better scalability.\n\n   - **Product**: The interface that defines the method that must be implemented by concrete products which the factory produces.\n   - **Concrete Product**: The concrete implementations of the product interface.\n   - **Creator**: An interface that declares the factory method.\n   - **",
